&lt;p&gt;The leaders of the United States, Russia and China signed a joint &amp;ldquo;peace declaration&amp;rdquo; at an unprecedented trilateral summit on Thursday, agreeing that nuclear war must never be fought. However, the high-profile meeting ended without any concrete commitment to the arms control negotiations that US&amp;nbsp;President Donald Trump has long advocated.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The declaration, signed by Trump, Chinese President Xi Jinping and Russian President Vladimir Putin, marked the culmination of Trump's three-day diplomatic tour of Asia.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;What Trump, Xi And Putin Agreed On&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The joint declaration outlined areas of agreement among the three nuclear powers, including the need to prevent nuclear conflict, expand petroleum production and reform international institutions.&lt;/p&gt;
